How to create server?
just like other object, we use yaml definition file to create service

High level structure of file remains same

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata: 
  name: myapp-service
  labels:
    app: front-service
spec: #this is where we define services, where it differs from other objects
  type : NodePort #type of service
  ports: 
    - targetPort: 80
      port: 80
      nodePort: 30008

      #out of these 3(targetport, port, nodeport), the only mandatory feild is port
      #if you dont provide target port it is assumed to be same as port
      #if you dont provide nodeport, a free port in valid range(30000-32767) is automatically allocated

  #ports section is an array/list, so you can have multiple such port mappings in a single service
  # - indicates first element

#now we need to conect service to the pod, here we just mentioned targetport but we didnt mentioned which pods targetport(there could be 100s of pods running with port 80)

#we will use labels and selectors to connect service and pods together
#selector will be inside spec
  
  selector: 
    app: redis
    type: database
    
    #mention the labels under selector section, whichever pods match those labels will connect to the service


"""
#pod definition file

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: redis-rc
  label: 
    app: redis
    type: database
spec:
  containers:
    - name: redis
      image: redis
"""

#now create the service
kubectl create -f service-definitio.yaml

#once the service is created, you can describe the server
kubectl describe service webapp-service

as per the labels in the pods and selector rule, the service will link to pods backend
you can see those IP of the pods and port of pod in endpoint section of the service

#to see the created services
kubectl get services

now you can connect the pod application using nodeport and node ip

so far it was service talking to single pod

in production we will have multiple pods running for same web application and function
# here the service when it is created, it will look for pods having same labels 
# the service then automatically selects all the pod endpoints to forward the external requests coming from the user

service uses "random" algorithm to send traffic to multiple pods connected to same port(service port)
session affinity: yes

thus service acts as built in load balancer to distribute traffic across pods

